--------------------------------------- Getting started ----------------------------------------------

- LET'S GENERATE THE PROJECT SKELETON, AND BEGIN OUR ADVENTURE!

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite Chapter 'From zero to something'

- In this first step, we will generate our project skeleton. The simplest way to do that is to use
    Angular CLI. ⚠️Note: If you already have installed Angular CLI, uninstall it with 
        
        npm uninstall -g @angular/cli.

1️⃣ Let’s start by installing this tool with:

        npm install -g @angular/cli@22.0.0
    
    his adds a new binary on your path named ng. You can check the version with:

        ng version
    
    This should display the same version as the one specified after the @ in the install command.
    If that’s not the case, check the FAQ section about installing and upgrading Angular CLI.



2️⃣ Generate the project
    Then go to a new empty folder and type:

        ng new ponyracer --defaults --no-routing --prefix pr --skip-install
    
    to generate a new project. After a few seconds (or even minutes), you should see a new folder
    named ponyracer (you may have a few warnings depending on your platform, nothing important).

    Run npm install to fetch the dependencies.

        npm install

    NPM might ask you if you want to run an audit command at the end of the installation: even if
    it’s tempting, don’t run it, because it may fetch incompatible versions with our project.
    If the installation fails because of an incompatible peer dependency, run the following
    configuration command:

        npm config set legacy-peer-deps=true --location=project
    
    then try the npm install command again.
    The Angular CLI generates a default Prettier configuration file (.prettierrc) when creating a
    new project. Replace it with the one provided in the resources to use our custom formatting
    rules.

    🔥The CLI used to come with e2e tests already configured with Protractor. Since Angular CLI v12,
    this is no longer the case. We need to pick the end-to-end testing library we want, and add it
    to our project.

    🔥We are going to use Playwright. We could set it up by hand, but the easiest way is to use a
    schematic. The Angular CLI is a very powerful tool. It allows third-party developers to build
    their own "extensions" of the CLI called "builders", and their own generators called "schematics".
    🔥This Playwright schematic is a schematic provided by the community that we’re going to use to
    add Playwright to our project:

        cd ponyracer
        ng add playwright-ng-schematics@21.1.0 --install-browsers --skip-confirmation

    If the installation fails because of an incompatible peer dependency, run:

        cd ponyracer
        npm config set legacy-peer-deps=true --location=project

    And try the ng add command again.

    🔥For a long time, the CLI included TSLint as well out of the box to check our code quality. But
    TSLint is now deprecated, so this is no longer the case since CLI v12. But no worries, we’re
    going to use ESLint instead.

        ng add angular-eslint@21.4.0 --skip-confirmation

    Everything is now installed, let’s go to the next step. Serve the application
   
        ng serve

    Then open your favorite browser and go to http://localhost:4200 You should see the home page
    displayed.



3️⃣ Add some styles
    Now let’s have a look at the code.
    Open the project with your favorite IDE or text editor. Visual Studio Code for example is free,
    available for all operating systems, and offers a very good TypeScript support. You can add the
    Angular Language Service plugin for the Angular support, and the ESLint plugin as well.

    Let’s start by making this application a bit prettier. I have poor CSS skills, so most of the
    time, I use the excellent Bootstrap framework to style my applications.

    First, 🔥let’s install bootstrap and fontawesome:

        npm install --save-exact bootstrap@5.3.8 @fortawesome/fontawesome-free@7.2.0

    🔥We now need to use these styles in our application. Open the file styles.css and add the
    following:
    styles.css:
        
        @import 'bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.css';
        @import '@fortawesome/fontawesome-free/css/all.css';
    
    Then, in the template of the root component, app.html, let’s replace the content with:
    app.html:

        <main class="container" style="margin-top: 70px;">
            <h1>Ponyracer</h1>
        </main>

    Restart the ng serve task, and your browser should refresh automatically and display our new
    template.
    The TypeScript configuration lives in tsconfig.json, and, as we saw, the compiler will help us
    to write good code. We can add a few options to make the compiler a bit stricter and catch more
    potential mistakes. So let’s replace the file generated by the CLI with the one in the resources.



4️⃣ Unit tests
    Now we just need to add the necessary to validate our exercises!
    🔥Let’s customize Vitest by adding a configuration file:

        ng generate config vitest
    
    🔥Then replace the generated vitest-base.config.ts file in the root of your application with the
    one provided in the resources.
    🔥Copy the tsconfig.spec.json file into the root of your application, 🔥and the setup-test.ts file
    into the src directory as well. This file configures the Vitest test environment: it adds a
    check to ensure that no console errors occur during tests, and extends Vitest locators with a
    utility method.
    
    🔥Let’s first check if the unit tests are OK. Create a results directory at the root of your
    application. Then replace app.spec.ts in your project (src/app) with the one provided, then run:

        ng test

    🔥By default, the tests are in "watch" mode, which means they will re-run on each file change. To
    launch the tests only once, you can run:

        ng test --no-watch
    
    🔥You can also display a UI with the --ui option.
    🔥The tests will also check the code coverage of your application. To run the tests with coverage,
        use the --coverage flag:

        ng test --coverage --no-watch

    🔥The coverage results are displayed in the terminal (text summary), and an HTML report is
    generated in the coverage/ directory. Open coverage/ponyracer/index.html in your browser to
    explore the detailed report.



5️⃣ End-to-end tests
    Now let’s see if the end-to-end tests are OK. Copy home.e2e-spec.ts in the e2e directory, and
    remove the default test example.spec.ts. Playwright has its own configuration file, replace
    playwright.config.ts (in the root directory) with the one provided. Also add the tsconfig.json
    file in the e2e directory provided in the resources.
    Then launch the tests with:

        ng e2e
    
    ⚠️Note:
    On some systems, you may need to run npx playwright install-deps to install missing system
    dependencies. You should have an explicit error message if this is the case.

    🔥If you want to have a UI to see the tests running, you can run the tests with:

        ng e2e --ui



6️⃣ Lint
    Last step, check the code quality. The packages are already installed, we just need to update
    the configuration file. Let’s replace the eslint.config.js file at the root of the project
    with the provided eslint.config.mjs file (note the different extension .mjs) and check the
    code quality with:

        ng lint

    Fix any error found (if any) to achieve 100%.

    🔥Note that you can run:

        ng lint --fix
    
    at any moment to automatically format your code, and get rid of annoying errors like missing
    whitespaces, semi-colons, etc.



